Cloud computing has become a normal part of my everyday life, even though I did not always realize that I was already using cloud services. Some of the cloud services and applications I regularly use are Google Drive, Netflix, and Messenger. These applications make it easier for me to store files, watch entertainment, and communicate with other people without depending entirely on the storage or resources of my own device. 

Google Drive is an example of SaaS (Software as a Service) because I can directly use its features, such as storing, accessing, and sharing files, without managing the underlying servers or infrastructure. It uses a Public Cloud deployment model because the service is provided over the internet to many users. Netflix can also be classified as SaaS since users can access its software and services through the internet without managing the system behind it. It is also considered a Public Cloud service because its cloud resources are available to the general public through its platform. Lastly, Messenger is another example of SaaS because I simply use the application to communicate without having to manage its servers or software infrastructure. It also follows a Public Cloud model since it is accessible to many users over the internet. 

For me, Git and GitHub are very important when working with cloud projects because cloud infrastructure can involve many files, configurations, and changes. Git allows developers to track modifications and return to an earlier version if something goes wrong. This is especially useful when a new change accidentally causes an error. GitHub also makes teamwork easier because multiple developers can work on the same project, review changes, and collaborate without constantly overwriting each other's work. 

Overall, I realized that cloud computing is already a big part of my daily activities. Learning how cloud models and version control work helps me better understand the technology behind the applications and systems I use every day. 
